   "url": "https://ukmt.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/KangPink20.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "9-10",
  "points": 6,
  "blankPoints": null,
  "wrongPoints": null,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "Eight consecutive three-digit positive integers have the following property: each of them is divisible by its last digit. What is the sum of the digits of the smallest of these eight integers?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "9"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "10"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "11"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "12"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "13"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "E",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Use the least common multiple",
    "body": "No number can end in 0, since it would have to be divisible by 0. Among eight consecutive integers the last digits are therefore $1,2,\\dots,8$ or $2,3,\\dots,9$, in order. Write the numbers as $\\overline{AB}n$. Divisibility of $\\overline{AB}n$ by $n$ is the same as divisibility of $\\overline{AB}0$ by $n$, since $\\overline{AB}n=\\overline{AB}0+n$.\n\nIf the last digits are $2,\\dots,9$, then $\\overline{AB}0$ must be a multiple of $\\text{lcm}(2,\\dots,9)=2520$, which is impossible for a three-digit number. So the last digits are $1,\\dots,8$ and $\\overline{AB}0$ is a multiple of $\\text{lcm}(1,\\dots,8)=840$. The only three-digit multiple is 840, so the numbers are $841,842,\\dots,848$.\n\nThe smallest is 841, with digit sum $8+4+1=13$.\n\n**Answer: (E) 13**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "number-theory"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
